            "kind": "agentStateChanged",
            "pane": "w1:p1",
            "state": "working"
          },
          {
            "kind": "agentStateChanged",
            "pane": "w1:p1",
            "state": "working"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expect": {
        "panes": [
          "w1:p1"
        ],
        "tabs": [],
        "workspaces": [],
        "agentStates": {
          "w1:p1": "working"
        },
        "focus": {},
        "health": "connected",
        "changes": [
          "agentStateChanged:w1:p1:idle->working"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "an agent state for an unknown pane is dropped, not invented",
      "why": "An agent event carries a pane id and nothing else - no tab, no workspace. A mirror that created a pane from one would hold an orphan the view cannot place in any tab, which is worse than missing it: the pane would appear nowhere and never leave. The snapshot after a reconnect is what fills real gaps.",
      "given": {
        "snapshot": {
          "panes": [
            {
              "id": "w1:p1",
              "tab": "w1:t1",
              "workspace": "w1",
              "agentState": "idle"
            }
          ]
        },
        "events": [
          {
            "kind": "agentStateChanged",
            "pane": "w1:p7",
            "state": "blocked"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expect": {
        "panes": [
          "w1:p1"
        ],
        "tabs": [],
        "workspaces": [],
        "agentStates": {
          "w1:p1": "idle"
        },
        "focus": {},
        "health": "connected",
        "changes": []
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "transitions that ran while nobody was listening are reported on reconnect",
      "why": "herdr stamps agent transitions from one session-wide counter, and that stamp reaches a client in exactly one place: the agents[] of a snapshot. It is never on an event. So a gap is visible only by comparing two snapshots against the transitions applied in between - here one was applied and the counter moved by four. Muster exists to route attention, so an agent that asked for the user during the gap has to be surfaced rather than quietly overwritten by the fresh snapshot.",
      "given": {
        "snapshot": {
          "panes": [
            {
              "id": "w1:p1",
              "tab": "w1:t1",
              "workspace": "w1",
              "agentState": "idle"
            }
          ],
          "agentStateSeq": 4
        },
        "events": [
          {
            "kind": "agentStateChanged",
            "pane": "w1:p1",
            "state": "working"
          }
        ],
        "resnapshot": {
          "panes": [
            {
              "id": "w1:p1",
              "tab": "w1:t1",
              "workspace": "w1",
              "agentState": "working"
            }
          ],
          "agentStateSeq": 9
        }
      },
      "expect": {
        "panes": [
          "w1:p1"
        ],
        "tabs": [],
        "workspaces": [],
        "agentStates": {
          "w1:p1": "working"
        },
        "focus": {},
        "health": "connected",
        "changes": [
          "agentStateChanged:w1:p1:idle->working",
          "agentTransitionsMissed:5..9"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a counter that moved by exactly what we applied is not a gap",
      "why": "The companion to the case above, because an over-eager gap detector is worse than none: it would claim a missed notification on every ordinary reconnect, and a warning that fires every time is one nobody reads. Two transitions applied, counter up by two, nothing missed.",
      "given": {
        "snapshot": {
          "panes": [
            {
              "id": "w1:p1",
              "tab": "w1:t1",
              "workspace": "w1",
              "agentState": "idle"
            }
